press control + arrowkey to move between spaces/words

ls - lists stuff

ls -l - lists the following: IsFolder, user/owner perms, group perms, other perms, number of hardlinks, file owner, file group, file size, modification time, filename

ln filename hardlinkpath - adds a hardlink to a file

ln -s targetfilename softlinkname - creates a softlink/shortcut

l - lists stuff a bit different, comes with extensions, example: "ls -la" displays hidden files and details/permissions

mkdir - creates a folder

touch - creates a file

touch -a -m -t (date, format yyyymmddhhmn.ms) (filename)
^modifies modification date of a file, don't need ()
^add -h to edit softlinks

rm - deletes files, "rm -r" deletes directories

rm -rf - force deletes any file you tell it to, very menacing command

cat - displays content of a file in terminal, "cat -n" displays it with line numbers very neat

&& - does nothing by itself but cna be used to chain/combo commands, 
    ex: "touch filename && cat filename" to create filename file and display it's contents(gonna be empty)

history - shows the history of all your terminal entries

vim - opens a file in a texteditor in the terminal itself in read mode, 
    in vim press i  to go into insert mode, lets you write stuff in the file

outside of insert mode: hold d to delete stuff quickly, press dd to delete an entire line at once, 
    press u to undo, press v to enter visual mode, lets you mark stuff with keyboard, or just do it with mouse

keybinds for moving through files: press "gg" to move to top of file, 
    press shift + g to move to bottom line of file, press shift + to move between spaces/words

keybind for selecting entire line and copying it(outside of insert mode): press "yy"

keybind for pasting: press "p" or hold it for many pastes

press escape to exit insert mode

press :q to save file content and exit vim

press :w to save file content

press :q to exit vim and go back to terminal, :q! to exit without saving, sometimes saves something as backup anyways xd
